// Deliberately empty, and the emptiness is the point.
//
// App is generated entirely from App.xaml: the XAML compiler emits App.xaml.g.h with the AppT<App>
// base, and nothing ever activates the class across an ABI, so it has no business in Windows
// metadata. Declaring it here as
//
//     runtimeclass App : Microsoft.UI.Xaml.Application { App(); }
//
// fails two ways over. MIDL rejects it outright, because a runtimeclass whose only member is a
// constructor has no members to put on an interface:
//
//     error MIDL5053: empty runtime class cannot have the [composable] or [activatable] attribute
//
// and adding [default_interface] to satisfy that only moves the failure: App then appears in the
// generated module.g.cpp, which calls winrt_make_WindowsTSPlayer_App() and so requires a
// factory_implementation::App that the XAML-generated headers do not define.
//
// MainWindow.idl is the contrasting case and does declare its class, because a Window genuinely is
// activated by name.
//
// The file itself stays, rather than being deleted, because the project references it and because
// the moment App grows anything projected, this is where it goes.

namespace WindowsTSPlayer
{
}
